WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:05.070
Turning the clock back to the 1940s and 50s,

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.770
the scientific community was in the midst of a debate about the nature of the hereditary 

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:13.990
material that is responsible for passing our traits on to our children,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.020
to our children's children, and so on for generations.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.210
Pretty existential question,

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:19.660
right?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.740
An experiment by Avery, MacLeod and McCarty in 1944 first 

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.590
suggested that proteins were not responsible for heredity as previously thought,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.350
and instead, DNA was the culprit.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:34.410
However,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.410
given that their results flew in the face of the current thinking,

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.240
not everyone was convinced.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:40.450
In science,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.230
a great deal of evidence is needed before a new scientific idea is accepted as 

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:45.820
fact.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:46.840
Therefore,

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:51.550
in 1952 Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase carried out experiments,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.350
which addressed a similar question.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.960
Was DNA the hereditary material,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:56.950
or was it protein,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.500
or was it something else?

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.630
To solve this important puzzle,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:05.070
Hershey and Chase needed a simple model organism to solve this complex 

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.810
question and a clever experiment.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.810
They chose the simplest organism possible,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:11.430
a bacteriophage,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.680
which is a virus that infects bacteria.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.850
This virus looks a bit like a lunar landing module,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.350
and attaches to the outside of the bacteria.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:25.590
It then injects a substance into the bacteria to create many new copies of the 

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:26.190
virus.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.210
These newly manufactured viruses are released when the bacteria bursts,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.230
so the substance injected inside of the bacteria must contain the instruction 

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.110
manual for making new virus.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.850
It contains the hereditary material.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.650
But what was this material that the virus injected into its host?

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.950
The answer to their question might shed light on the nature of heredity for all life on Earth.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.940
Viruses consist of two things:

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.950
a DNA core surrounded by a protein coat.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:02.100
Hershey and Chase needed to figure out which of the two was injected into the 

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:02.750
bacteria.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.190
They ran two experiments,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:05.970
and in each,

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.470
they labeled one of the components. For this labeling they used radioactive 

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.120
tags,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.840
radioactive sulfur for the protein and radioactive phosphate 

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.100
for the DNA.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:23.050
This was an elegant method because phosphate is only found in DNA and not 

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.490
in the amino acids that make up proteins.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:29.400
And sulfur is only found in proteins and not DNA.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:35.110
After the virus has infected the bacteria, the researchers examined whether the 

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.810
radioactive signal was detected inside the bacteria or left 

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.360
outside. To do so,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.160
they needed to separate the bacteria from the viruses attached to their outsides and 

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.060
did so using a Waring blender, similar to that used for making milkshakes.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:53.950
Once separated,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.650
they then centrifuged the samples.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.200
Since bacteria are bigger,

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:59.030
they were centrifuged to the bottom,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.970
while the smaller viruses remained at the top of the tube.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.420
When they prepared virus with sulfur labeled protein and mixed it with bacteria,

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.660
they found that most of the radioactivity remained with the virus and did not 

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:18.450
enter the bacteria. When they allowed the virus to replicate and collected the 

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:23.410
new viruses, less than 1% of the protein radioactivity from the 

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.210
viral parents were found in the newly replicated virus,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.880
so the protein was not being inherited.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:31.640
On the other hand,

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:36.540
when they performed the same experiment using virus with its DNA labeled with phosphate,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.760
they found that most of the radioactive DNA was injected into the bacteria from 

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.980
the attacking virus,

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.720
and much of this labeled DNA was inherited and passed on to the new virus 

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:48.370
generation.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.600
Hershey and Chase added to the mounting evidence,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.950
pointing the finger at DNA as the hereditary material.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.910
But they were aware that their experiments left a few unanswered questions, as 

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.250
experiments often do,

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.790
and they were cautious in their conclusions. From their observations,

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:11.560
they stated that DNA had some function in the multiplication of the virus,

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:16.530
whereas proteins did not. Word about the Hershey-Chase experiment spread 

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:21.440
quickly and helped to convince many scientists that DNA indeed was 

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.060
the molecule of heredity,

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.950
and the remaining skeptics could not hold up for much longer.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:28.440
A year later,

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.900
Watson and Crick published their model of the DNA double helix,

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.420
which provided an elegant explanation for how DNA,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.260
with such a simple structure,

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:41.350
could be replicated and pass along information from parents to offspring.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.870
The Hershey-Chase experiment is considered a classic in biology because it 

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:51.820
tackled an important question with the use of a well chosen model organism 

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:56.650
and an elegant experimental design in order to clearly distinguish between 

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.350
two competing hypotheses.

